//? Title: ChaCha20 as a Buffered Random Engine
//? Subtitle: Word order, reproducible draws, copied state and checked fixed-nonce exhaustion
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The random-engine interface turns ChaCha20's 64-byte blocks into successive
64-bit draws and common distribution samples. That wrapper needs a precise
contract: how words are packed, which unused values stay buffered, how much
capacity remains, and what happens at the final block counter. We inspect
actual native draws, reproduce the original public-key example, and consume
the final permitted block. The canonical engine now shares its rounds with
the byte-oriented cipher and rejects exhaustion without changing its nonce.
Seed expansion and copied engines provide reproducibility, with no added
entropy or independence guarantee.
)

\toc

> One published block becomes eight raw draws

ChaCha20 has sixteen 32-bit state words: constants, eight key words, a block
counter and three nonce words. Twenty rounds and feed-forward produce sixteen
output words. The engine returns pairs in little-endian order: the first word
occupies the low half of a uint64 and the second occupies the high half.
Eight calls consume a block. The ninth generates the next counter's block;
it does not discard the first block's buffered tail after each call.

Use RFC 8439 Section 2.3.2. The first four key bytes 00 01 02 03 are the
word 03020100, not 00010203. The public nonce's first four bytes 00 00 00 09
become 09000000. Word-valued constructor arguments therefore work identically
on either host byte order. No pointer reinterpretation is needed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chacha20_engine_buffer.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::array<std::uint32_t,8> key{0x03020100,0x07060504,0x0b0a0908,0x0f0e0d0c,
      0x13121110,0x17161514,0x1b1a1918,0x1f1e1d1c};
  dm::random::ChaCha20 engine(key,{0x09000000,0x4a000000,0},1);
  const auto draws=dm::random::chacha20_trace(engine,10);
  std::array<std::uint8_t,32> bytes{};std::iota(bytes.begin(),bytes.end(),std::uint8_t{0});
  const auto block=dm::algorithms::chacha20_block_trace(bytes,1,{0,0,0,9,0,0,0,0x4a,0,0,0,0});
  bool matched=true;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<8;++i) {
    const auto expected=std::uint64_t(block.feed_forward[2*i])|(std::uint64_t(block.feed_forward[2*i+1])<<32);
    matched=matched && draws[i].value==expected;
  }
  if(!matched || draws[8].counter!=2 || draws[8].word_index!=0) return 1;
  std::print("| draw | block counter | uint64 index | value (hex) | raw draws before |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<draws.size();++i) {
    const auto& d=draws[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:016x} | {} |\n",i+1,d.counter,d.word_index,d.value,d.remaining_before);
  }
  std::print("\nAll eight RFC block words match: {}. Remaining raw draws: {}.\n",matched,engine.remaining_u64());
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=5.2;node.height=2.4;node.font_size=18;
  diagram.node("block",{0,0},"RFC counter 1\n20 rounds and feed-forward\n64 output bytes",node)
      .node("buffer",{8,0},"buffer: 16 x uint32\nretain the unused words\n8 x uint64 draws",node);
  node.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("first",{8,-4},std::format("draw 1: counter {}, word {}\n{:016x}\nlow e4e7f110, high 15593bd1",draws[0].counter,draws[0].word_index,draws[0].value),node)
      .node("ninth",{0,-4},std::format("draw 9: counter {}, word {}\n{:016x}\nrefill after eight draws",draws[8].counter,draws[8].word_index,draws[8].value),node)
      .edge("block","buffer","generate")
      .edge("buffer","first","pair low / high")
      .edge("first","ninth","consume words 1..7")
      .text({4,2.5},"actual raw draws; key and nonce are public conformance inputs",18)
      .save_svg("build/chacha20_engine_buffer.svg");
}
```
