All eight RFC block words match: true. Remaining raw draws: 34359738350.
\image(figures/chacha20_engine_buffer.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The wrapper retains unused words and generates the next counter's block when its buffer is empty.)
\alttext(A counter-one block fills sixteen 32-bit buffer words. The first raw draw pairs the first two words; after eight draws the ninth starts counter two.)

For block words w0 through w15, draw j is w(2j)+2^32*w(2j+1),
for j=0 through 7. Its least-significant eight bytes are the original next
eight keystream bytes. The displayed first value 15593bd1e4e7f110 therefore
corresponds to RFC bytes 10 f1 e7 e4 d1 3b 59 15. Reformatting a numeric
uint64 in hexadecimal is not the same as printing the bytes in stream order.
The companion [cipher analysis](../../algorithms/cryptography/chacha20_analysis.html)
checks the full published byte vectors and actual internal round states.

> Preserve the original example and account for sampler consumption

The original example uses a deliberately public eight-word key, nonce [7,0,0]
and starting counter zero. Keep that sequence exactly: a raw draw, then
uniform01(), then an inclusive bounded integer. Each sampler advances the
same raw stream; they are not three separate experiments from its beginning.
The original stored bounded result was stale: the current common sampler uses
high-product rejection and returns 6 here. The raw value and six-place uniform
value remain unchanged; this conversion does not alter the shared sampler.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::array<std::uint32_t,8> key{0x11111111,0x22222222,0x33333333,0x44444444,
      0x55555555,0x66666666,0x77777777,0x88888888};
  dm::random::ChaCha20 engine(key,{7,0,0});
  const auto before=engine.remaining_u64();
  const auto raw=engine.next_u64();const auto after_raw=engine.remaining_u64();
  const auto uniform=engine.uniform01();const auto after_uniform=engine.remaining_u64();
  const auto bounded=engine.uniform_u64(1,6);const auto after_bounded=engine.remaining_u64();
  if(raw!=6902715600735840086ULL || bounded!=6) return 1;
  std::print("| call | value | raw words consumed |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| next_u64 | {} | {} |\n",raw,before-after_raw);
  std::print("| uniform01 | {:.6f} | {} |\n",uniform,after_raw-after_uniform);
  std::print("| uniform_u64(1,6) | {} | {} |\n",bounded,after_uniform-after_bounded);
  std::print("\nRaw capacity left: {}.\n",after_bounded);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| call | value | raw words consumed |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| next_u64 | 6902715600735840086 | 1 |
| uniform01 | 0.404146 | 1 |
| uniform_u64(1,6) | 6 | 1 |

Raw capacity left: 34359738365.
// result_end

For a raw word X, uniform01() uses U=floor(X/2^11)/2^53. It returns
one of 2^53 grid points in [0,1), consumes one word and never returns one.
The bounded-integer sampler multiplies by the interval size and rejects
selected low products before returning the high half. Under uniform raw
words, that avoids modulo bias. Rejection can consume several raw words;
the displayed one-word use is specific to this call, not a universal bound.
Other samplers can cache outputs: a cached normal draw can even survive raw
stream exhaustion. Remaining capacity therefore counts next_u64() calls,
not arbitrary distribution calls. Floating transformations depend on the
platform's math implementation; exact raw integer replay has a stronger contract.

> Stop at the fixed nonce's final block

A starting counter c allows B=2^32-c blocks and 8B raw uint64 draws.
The default zero counter allows 2^35 draws, or 2^38 raw bytes. These are
primitive stream limits, not the data limit of a separate authenticated
ChaCha20-Poly1305 construction. A nonce is fixed for an engine's lifetime.
The old carry from the wrapped counter into a nonce word is removed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/chacha20_engine_limit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::random::ChaCha20 engine(std::array<std::uint32_t,8>{},{},0xffffffffU);
  const auto initial=engine.remaining_u64();
  bool preflight=false;try {(void)dm::random::chacha20_trace(engine,9);}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {preflight=true;}
  const auto unchanged=engine.remaining_u64()==initial;
  const auto first=dm::random::chacha20_trace(engine,1);
  const auto after_first=engine.remaining_u64();
  const auto tail=dm::random::chacha20_trace(engine,7);
  bool exhausted=false;try {(void)engine.next_u64();}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {exhausted=true;}
  if(!preflight || !unchanged || !exhausted || engine.remaining_u64()!=0) return 1;
  std::print("Nine-word trace rejected before mutation: {}. Capacity unchanged: {}.\n",preflight,unchanged);
  std::print("\n| final block | first word index | last word index | consumed uint64s | remaining |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",first[0].counter,first[0].word_index,tail.back().word_index,first.size()+tail.size(),engine.remaining_u64());
  std::print("\nNinth raw draw throws: {}. Empty trace at exhaustion has {} records.\n",exhausted,dm::random::chacha20_trace(engine,0).size());
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=5.2;node.height=2.2;node.font_size=18;
  diagram.node("start",{0,0},std::format("counter = 4294967295\n{} raw draws available\nblock not yet generated",initial),node)
      .node("tail",{8,0},std::format("after draw 1\n{} raw draws buffered\nnonce unchanged",after_first),node);
  node.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("empty",{8,-4},"after draw 8\n0 raw draws remain\nno counter wrap",node);
  node.fill={254,226,226};
  diagram.node("error",{0,-4},"draw 9 throws length_error\n0 raw draws remain\nstate unchanged",node)
      .edge("start","tail","one draw").edge("tail","empty","seven draws")
      .edge("empty","error","attempt another draw")
      .text({4,2.3},"complete-call trace preflight: request 9 from capacity 8 rejects before drawing",18)
      .save_svg("build/chacha20_engine_limit.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Nine-word trace rejected before mutation: true. Capacity unchanged: true.

| final block | first word index | last word index | consumed uint64s | remaining |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 4294967295 | 0 | 7 | 8 | 0 |

Ninth raw draw throws: true. Empty trace at exhaustion has 0 records.
\image(figures/chacha20_engine_limit.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The final counter's buffered tail remains available; the following raw draw fails without switching nonce.)
\alttext(Eight raw words are available initially, seven after the first draw, and zero after eight. The ninth draw throws and leaves the exhausted state unchanged.)

After a refill, let N be the number of not-yet-generated blocks and let t
be the number of buffered uint64 values. Then remaining_u64()=8N+t.
Generating the final block sets N to zero but t to eight. This distinction
prevents both premature rejection and accidental counter wrap. Trace inspection
checks the entire requested count and reserves its records before drawing, so
an over-capacity request yields no partial trace and leaves raw and cached state
unchanged. Ordinary distribution calls have no such whole-call preflight:
for example, a rejection sampler may consume its last word and then throw.

> Seed expansion and copies reproduce streams

The one-integer constructor is for deterministic simulation. Four SplitMix64
outputs become eight low/high 32-bit key words, with nonce and counter zero.
The new native seed-key helper exposes that existing packing without duplicating
it in the report. Expansion does not increase entropy: at most 2^64 keys are
reachable from a 64-bit seed, despite the 256-bit destination array.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto zero=dm::random::chacha20_seed_key(0);
  std::print("| SplitMix draw | low key word | high key word |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i) std::print("| {} | {:08x} | {:08x} |\n",i+1,zero[2*i],zero[2*i+1]);
  dm::random::ChaCha20 seeded(42),explicit_key(dm::random::chacha20_seed_key(42));
  bool same=true;for(int i=0;i<24;++i) same=same && seeded.next_u64()==explicit_key.next_u64();
  (void)seeded.normal();auto copy=seeded;
  const bool cached_same=seeded.normal()==copy.normal();
  const auto a=dm::random::chacha20_trace(seeded,17),b=dm::random::chacha20_trace(copy,17);
  bool copied_same=true;for(std::size_t i=0;i<a.size();++i)copied_same=copied_same && a[i].value==b[i].value;
  if(!same || !cached_same || !copied_same) return 1;
  std::print("\nSeed constructor equals explicit expanded key for 24 draws: {}.\n",same);
  std::print("Copied normal cache matches: {}. Copied raw tail and following blocks match: {}.\n",cached_same,copied_same);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| SplitMix draw | low key word | high key word |
| ---: | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | 7b1dcdaf | e220a839 |
| 2 | a1b965f4 | 6e789e6a |
| 3 | 8009454f | 06c45d18 |
| 4 | 724c81ec | f88bb8a8 |

Seed constructor equals explicit expanded key for 24 draws: true.
Copied normal cache matches: true. Copied raw tail and following blocks match: true.
// result_end

Copying preserves the partial raw block and inherited normal cache. It duplicates
the stream at that point; it does not create independent random streams. This
wrapper does not acquire operating-system entropy, enforce nonce uniqueness,
authenticate data, erase keys, reseed after compromise or provide a cryptographic
random-service lifecycle. The public keys here establish reproducibility and
conformance only. No observational dataset is needed for those exact contracts.

The native tests compare 1,000 random key/nonce streams, including partial buffers
and near-exhaustion counters, against a separate block oracle that implements
diagonal rounds through row rotations. They check every recorded value, location
and remaining count, all final-buffer tails, failed preflight, copied caches,
1,000 seed expansions and the recomputed sampler outputs. The existing cipher
vectors and common-engine sampler tests remain regression checks. Shared rounds
remove algorithm duplication; conformance tests and a short report are not a
security proof or a machine-code timing audit.

> References

- Y. Nir and A. Langley (2018). [RFC 8439: ChaCha20 and Poly1305 for IETF Protocols](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8439.html). Sections 2.3–2.4 define the byte primitive; uint64 buffering and checked engine exhaustion are native wrapper behavior.
- S. Vigna. [SplitMix64 reference implementation](https://prng.di.unimi.it/splitmix64.c). The library's legacy seed constructor uses four outputs in low/high order.
